<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Galicia</h1><p class="st-meta">910 CE – 1833 CE (approx.) · capital Santiago de Compostela</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The north-western Iberian kingdom centred on Santiago de Compostela, sometimes ruled by its own kings but usually joined with León and Castile.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-iii-of-asturias">Alfonso III of Asturias</a> was deposed in 910, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordono-ii-of-leon">Ordoño II</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria">Galicia</a> as his kingdom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> had been the core of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-the-suebi">Kingdom of the Suebi</a> and a part of the Asturian realm, and the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-the-great">St James</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Compostela</a> had made it famous across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria">Galicia</a> was dominated by a powerful nobility and the church, above all the archbishops of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-jamaica">Santiago</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-gelmirez">Diego Gelmírez</a>. It had its own kings at times, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garcia-ii-of-galicia">García II</a> from 1065 to 1071, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII</a> was crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> in 1111. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galician-portuguese">Galician-Portuguese</a> became a major literary language, used for the troubadour lyric and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantigas-de-santa-maria">Cantigas de Santa Maria</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1230 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria">Galicia</a> was permanently part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile</a>, though it kept the title of kingdom and from the 16th century its own Junta. The provincial reform of 1833 abolished it as an administrative unit, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> today is an autonomous community.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-galicia.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Galicia" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Instituto Gallaecia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bandeira_Hist%C3%B3rica_Galega_do_Escudo_de_Armas_do_Reino_da_Galiza.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>910 CE – 1833 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santiago de Compostela</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ordoño II, García II, Alfonso VII</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Galician-Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>, <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Regnum Galleciae</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1372&amp;focus=kingdom-of-galicia" aria-label="Kingdom of Galicia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4474 655 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Galicia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4794 911L4795 953M4794 911L4865 919M4794 911L4885 950M4794 911L4909 989M4794 911L4798 956"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4795 953h0M4865 919h0M4885 950h0M4909 989h0M4798 956h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4794 911h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4794 911h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Santiago de Compostela. <a href="/atlas?year=1372&amp;focus=kingdom-of-galicia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
